{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Asymmetric attestation foundation — DSSE envelope + SSHSIG scheme + pluggable-scheme substrate\n\n## Context\nrebar signs its attestations (plan-review, completion-verifier verdicts) with a **symmetric HMAC**\nkeyed on a per-environment secret (`src/rebar/signing.py`; `.signing-key` / `REBAR_SIGNING_KEY`).\nTwo upcoming capabilities — per-person **authorship** signing (the identity epic) and\nthird-party-verifiable **operation certificates** (the op-cert epic) — both need **asymmetric**\nsignatures (verify-by-anyone, forge-by-none) over attestation bytes, verified offline. Building\nthat signing layer twice would duplicate security-critical code and worsen the namespace collision\nalready forming on the `SIGNATURE` event type / `signature` / `key_id` fields. **Beneficiary:** the\ntwo consumer epics (and their end users) — they get one hardened, standards-based signing substrate\ninstead of two divergent stacks.\n\n## Approach (and why)\nReuse established standards rather than hand-roll crypto:\n- **DSSE (Dead Simple Signing Envelope)** as the envelope, signing `PAE(payloadType, body)` over\n  exact opaque bytes. PAE eliminates the JSON re-serialization / canonicalization attack class (we\n  never re-serialize parsed JSON to verify) and carries **no in-band `alg`** the verifier trusts —\n  closing the JWT `alg`-confusion class (RFC 8725). DSSE is the Sigstore/in-toto/SLSA standard;\n  ~30 lines, offline, scheme-agnostic.\n- **SSHSIG via OpenSSH `ssh-keygen -Y`** (stdlib `subprocess`, **no new core dependency**) as the\n  first asymmetric scheme — reuses the reference implementation and developers' existing SSH keys;\n  verified offline against an `allowed_signers`-format trust input. Fail-closed was confirmed\n  experimentally (wrong namespace / unknown principal / substituted key / tampered bytes / expired\n  window all exit non-zero; `-Y check-novalidate` is structure-only and is never used for authz).\n- **Pluggable scheme registry + per-kind policy table** (`kind → {scheme, namespace, trust-root}`),\n  pinned out-of-band; the verifier **never** selects the scheme from record data. The existing\n  **HMAC is registered as a legacy scheme** so current attestations keep verifying unchanged (this\n  is the expand phase; the HMAC→asymmetric cutover for operation-certs is the op-cert epic's\n  contract phase).\n\nAlternative considered and rejected: `securesystemslib` for signing — it has **no SSH signer** (so\nit would not cover the authorship path) and pulls `cryptography` (heavy) for Ed25519; core stays\nstdlib-only.\n\n## Scope\n- **In:** a new attestation-substrate module (DSSE envelope + PAE; scheme registry; SSHSIG scheme\n  via subprocess; per-kind policy table; verify-dispatch with domain separation); registration of\n  the existing HMAC as a legacy scheme; an ADR; unit/behavioral tests.\n- **Out:** any identity entity or authorship kind (identity epic); any change to operation-cert\n  behavior / actor / enforcement (op-cert epic); **removing** HMAC (op-cert epic's contract phase).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A new module (e.g. `src/rebar/attest/`) exposes `sign(kind, body_bytes) -> envelope` and\n      `verify(kind, envelope, trust_root) -> verdict`, where `kind` selects `{scheme, namespace,\n      trust-root}` from an out-of-band policy table (not from the envelope). A unit test asserts a\n      record whose in-band scheme field is tampered still verifies under the policy-pinned scheme\n      (no `alg` confusion).\n- [ ] DSSE PAE is implemented per the v1.0.0 spec\n      (`PAE(type,body)=\"DSSEv1\" SP len(type) SP type SP len(body) SP body`), proven by a unit test\n      against the spec's worked example; a test with duplicate/re-ordered JSON keys demonstrates\n      verify operates on the exact stored bytes and does not re-serialize.\n- [ ] The SSHSIG scheme signs and verifies via `ssh-keygen -Y` in a subprocess using argv arrays\n      (no shell), branches only on exit code, and allowlist-validates namespace + principal. A test\n      matrix asserts fail-closed on: tampered bytes, wrong namespace, unknown principal, substituted\n      key, and expired validity window.\n- [ ] The existing HMAC signer is registered as a legacy scheme; a plan-review/completion-verifier\n      attestation created before this change still returns verdict `certified` (regression test).\n- [ ] `ssh-keygen` availability/version (>= 8.9 for validity intervals) is detected; its absence\n      fails closed with a clear error (test asserts no silent pass).\n- [ ] An ADR under `docs/adr/` records the substrate: DSSE envelope, pluggable scheme, per-kind\n      policy table, domain separation, HMAC-as-legacy.\n- [ ] One signing substrate exists that both consumer epics import; no consumer needs its own crypto.\n- [ ] Signatures are produced/verified over DSSE-PAE bytes; verification never re-serializes parsed JSON\n- [ ] The SSHSIG scheme signs/verifies offline via `ssh-keygen` with **no new core runtime dependency**\n- [ ] Existing plan-review / completion-verifier attestations still verify (HMAC as a registered legacy\n\n## Intended decomposition (children)\n1. DSSE envelope + PAE module (+ spec-example + duplicate-key tests).\n2. Scheme registry + per-kind policy table + verify-dispatch (domain separation; alg-confusion test).\n3. SSHSIG scheme via `ssh-keygen` (fail-closed subprocess contract + test matrix).\n4. Register existing HMAC as a legacy scheme (regression test).\n5. ADR + docs.\n\n## Prior art / grounding\nDSSE protocol v1.0.0 (secure-systems-lab/dsse); in-toto attestation; OpenSSH PROTOCOL.sshsig +\n`allowed_signers`; git SSH commit signing (`gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile`); RFC 8725 (JWT BCP);\nRFC 8785 (JCS, considered and rejected in favour of DSSE PAE). Fail-closed behavior of\n`ssh-keygen -Y verify` confirmed by direct experiment in this environment (OpenSSH 10.2)."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785007075853725001,"uuid":"ac42e983-297b-40b7-92c5-a57958266120"}